    <h1>AI Privacy Policy Generator: What an AI or LLM App Has to Disclose</h1>
    <p class="last-updated">Published November 27, 2026 &middot; Updated November 27, 2026</p>

    <p>A privacy policy for an AI app is an ordinary app privacy policy plus five disclosures that model-backed features add: what users type, upload or say to the model and what it returns; which model provider receives that content; whether anyone trains on it; how long prompts and conversation logs are kept; and whether the AI makes decisions about people. Any chatbot or assistant that sends user input to a hosted model has to answer all five.</p>

    <p>To draft one, open the <a href="https://buildprivacypolicy.com/">free privacy policy generator</a> and pick the closest <strong>App profile</strong>, or <strong>Custom / not sure</strong>. Under <strong>Data Collection</strong>, add <strong>AI features</strong> to "Why is this data used?" and <strong>AI provider</strong> to "Who receives this data?", then tick <strong>AI Provider</strong> under <strong>Third-party SDKs / services</strong>. In <strong>High-Risk Checks</strong>, the <strong>AI features</strong> question asks which case you are in: <strong>No AI</strong>, <strong>On-device only</strong>, <strong>AI provider, no training</strong>, <strong>AI provider, may train</strong> or <strong>Own model training</strong>. The sections below explain what each answer has to cover.</p>

    <nav class="toc">
      <p class="toc-title">Contents</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#what">What an AI App Discloses That an Ordinary App Does Not</a></li>
        <li><a href="#prompts">Prompts and Outputs as Personal Data</a></li>
        <li><a href="#providers">Third-Party Model Providers as Sub-Processors</a></li>
